`,s.jsx(S,{items:[{q:"Explain checkerboard artefacts in one sentence, and give the condition that prevents them.",a:"When kernel size is not divisible by stride, interior output cells receive different numbers of overlapping kernel stamps — a count that is periodic with period s — so some cells are systematically larger and a grid texture appears. The condition is k mod s = 0, which makes every interior cell receive exactly (k/s)² stamps."},{q:"Why is the artefact described as geometric rather than learned?",a:"Because it follows purely from which output positions the stamps land on, which is fixed by k, s and p — it is present at random initialisation, before any training. The network can partially suppress it by learning compensating kernel values, but it spends capacity doing so, which is why choosing compatible k and s (or resize-then-convolve) is the better fix."}]}),`
